<!-- PART:PREFACE -->
<section class="part" id="preface">
  <h1 class="part-title">Preface — How to Use This Book</h1>
  <p>You are holding a compressed field course in creating AI models: seven chapters that take you from a single perceptron to a transformer trained on GPUs, and then past the loss curve to a monitored, governed production system. It assumes you can code a little and are willing to be precise about the rest. Everything else is taught in place.</p>
  <p>The book is organized in four movements:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Chapters 1–2</strong> establish what a model is and the shapes it can take — the vocabulary of architectures.</li>
    <li><strong>Chapters 3–4</strong> cover the discipline around the model — data preparation and the measurement system that tells you whether it is any good.</li>
    <li><strong>Chapters 5–6</strong> explain how models learn (optimization, regularization, scaling) and what happens after training (serving, drift, retraining, governance).</li>
    <li><strong>Chapter 7</strong> lays the whole syllabus out at a glance, with a reading list and a path forward.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Six dashed boxes marked <strong>Exercise</strong> are woven through the chapters. They are not optional reading; they are the course. A model you have not trained yourself is a word you have only heard.</p>
  <div class="callout key">
    <span class="ct">A note on editions</span>
    <p>This file is the single source of truth for the book. In the web companion (<em>Curriculum</em>) the threshold trade-off in §4.6 is an interactive gauge you can drag; here it is a static table — same numbers, less friction. To convert this file into EPUB or MOBI, see the accompanying README, or run <code>make_epub.py</code> for a dependency-free build.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- PART:CH1 -->
<section class="part chapter" id="ch1">
  <p class="chap-kicker">Chapter 1</p>
  <h1 class="chap-title">Foundations</h1>
  <p class="chap-lede">Every model is a function waiting to be shaped. Machine learning reframes a problem: instead of writing the rules, you write a parameterized function and let data determine its parameters.</p>

  <h2>1.1 What a model actually is</h2>
  <p>A model is a <em>family</em> of functions. A linear model is <code>ŷ = w·x + b</code>: the family is all lines, and the parameters (<code>w</code>, <code>b</code>) pick one specific line. A neural network is the same idea taken to the extreme — a function whose shape is so flexible that, given enough width and depth, it can approximate essentially any mapping. Training is the process of navigating parameter space until the function behaves the way you want. Everything else in this book — architectures, data pipelines, metrics — exists in service of that one act: fitting <code>f(x)</code> so that <code>f(x) ≈ y</code>.</p>
  <p>In practice, building a model is a six-step workflow. Keep the shape of it in mind; every later chapter is a zoom into one of these steps.</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Collect.</strong> Raw signals — logs, images, text, sensor streams.</li>
    <li><strong>Structure.</strong> Clean, label, and split into train / validation / test.</li>
    <li><strong>Choose.</strong> Pick an architecture suited to the data (Chapter 2).</li>
    <li><strong>Fit.</strong> Optimize parameters against a loss function (Chapter 5).</li>
    <li><strong>Evaluate.</strong> Measure on held-out data. Iterate honestly (Chapter 4).</li>
    <li><strong>Ship.</strong> Deploy, monitor, and retrain as the world shifts (Chapter 6).</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>1.2 The three learning paradigms</h2>
  <p>How does the model learn? Three regimes, distinguished by what signal the data provides:</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Paradigm</th><th>What the model sees</th><th>Where it shines</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>Supervised</b></td><td>Labeled pairs (x, y); the model is graded on every prediction</td><td>Classification and regression — the workhorse of most applications, and the setting where most of this book lives</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Unsupervised</b></td><td>Unlabeled data; the model must find structure</td><td>Clustering, embeddings, dimensionality reduction — and crucially, the self-supervised pretraining that powers modern AI</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Reinforcement</b></td><td>Delayed reward signals for sequences of actions</td><td>Robotics, game play, and preference-based alignment (RLHF) for language models</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <div class="callout key">
    <span class="ct">In practice</span>
    <p>State-of-the-art systems rarely pick just one. A typical large language model is <strong>self-supervised</strong> (predict the next token) for most of its compute, then <strong>supervised fine-tuned</strong> on demonstrations, then <strong>reinforcement-tuned</strong> against human preferences. Paradigms stack; the fundamentals do not change.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>1.3 Loss, gradients, and the engine of learning</h2>
  <p>Training needs three ingredients: a <strong>loss function</strong> that scores how wrong the model is, a <strong>differentiable path</strong> from parameters to that score, and an <strong>optimizer</strong> that walks the parameters downhill. The magic ingredient is the <em>chain rule</em> — backpropagation — which computes the gradient of the loss with respect to <em>every</em> parameter in a single backward pass.</p>
<pre><span class="cm"># the entire training loop, honestly compressed</span>
<span class="kw">for</span> batch <span class="kw">in</span> dataloader:
    x, y = batch
    y_hat = model(x)              <span class="cm"># forward pass</span>
    loss  = criterion(y_hat, y)   <span class="cm"># how wrong?</span>
    loss.backward()               <span class="cm"># backprop: ∂loss/∂θ</span>
    optimizer.step()              <span class="cm"># nudge parameters</span>
    optimizer.zero_grad()</pre>
  <p>Autograd frameworks (PyTorch, JAX, TensorFlow) build a computation graph during the forward pass and apply the chain rule automatically. Your job is to design the graph; the framework does the calculus.</p>

  <h2>1.4 Choosing the right tool</h2>
  <p>Not every problem deserves a neural network. A first mapping from task shape to starting point:</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Task shape</th><th>Start here</th><th>Reach for instead</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>Structured table, clean columns</b></td><td>Gradient boosting (XGBoost, LightGBM)</td><td>Logistic / linear regression, small MLPs</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Images, video, raw pixels</b></td><td>Convolutional networks</td><td>ViT-style transformers, diffusion models</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Text and language</b></td><td>A pretrained transformer</td><td>Fine-tune an LLM — a smaller one first</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Sequences with local patterns</b></td><td>RNN / GRU / small CNN</td><td>Transformer encoder (attention wins at scale)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Decisions over time</b></td><td>Value methods, policy gradients</td><td>PPO with a learned reward model</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="exercise">
    <span class="etag">Exercise 01</span>
    <ol>
      <li>Take the iris dataset and fit <code>sklearn.linear_model.LogisticRegression</code>.</li>
      <li>Print <code>coef_</code> and <code>intercept_</code> — these <em>are</em> the learned function. Which feature dominates for each class?</li>
      <li>Now fit the same model on 30 samples. Compare the coefficients. What does that instability teach you about data scale?</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- PART:CH2 -->
<section class="part chapter" id="ch2">
  <p class="chap-kicker">Chapter 2</p>
  <h1 class="chap-title">Architectures</h1>
  <p class="chap-lede">The anatomy of a model: classifiers to transformers. An architecture is a bet about structure — each family assumes something about how the data is organized, and encodes that assumption in its wiring.</p>

  <h2>2.1 Linear models and the perceptron</h2>
  <p>The <strong>perceptron</strong> is the atom of deep learning: a dot product plus a threshold. Keep it linear and you have regression; stack layers of it and you have a network. Linear models remain a first resort because they are <em>interpretable</em> — each weight is a direct statement about a feature — and they anchor the intuition: every nonlinearity you add later is a controlled departure from this simple case.</p>
  <p>They endure for good reasons: fast to fit, cheap to serve, no hyperparameter maze, and coefficients that read like a report. Logistic regression is still the baseline that deep models must beat to earn their GPU time. But a straight hyperplane cannot carve XOR-shaped decision boundaries, and the feature engineering needed to bend it becomes unmanageable. That gap is exactly what nonlinear models exist to fill.</p>

  <h2>2.2 Feedforward networks (MLPs)</h2>
  <p>A multilayer perceptron chains a small set of operations, repeated:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Input.</strong> A raw feature vector <code>x ∈ ℝⁿ</code> — flattened, scaled, ready.</li>
    <li><strong>Linear.</strong> <code>z = Wx + b</code> — a learned projection into a new geometry.</li>
    <li><strong>Nonlinear.</strong> <code>σ(z)</code> — ReLU or GELU. Without it, stacking layers is just one big matrix multiplication.</li>
    <li><strong>Normalize.</strong> BatchNorm or LayerNorm — keeps activations in a trainable range.</li>
    <li><strong>Head.</strong> The task output — class logits, a scalar, or a probability vector.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The universal approximation theorem says one hidden layer can approximate any continuous function — in practice you need <em>many</em> layers, which is why the field went deep. The subtlety: more layers and width buy expressivity, but only the <em>right</em> initialization, normalization, and optimizer make that capacity learnable. Architecture is a contract between capacity and optimization.</p>

  <h2>2.3 CNNs — vision's local experts</h2>
  <p>Convolutional networks encode a powerful prior: <strong>locality and translation invariance</strong>. Kernels slide across the input, reusing the same weights, building edge → texture → part → object hierarchies while pooling layers shrink spatial dimensions and enlarge receptive fields.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>What it does</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>Conv layer</b></td><td>Sliding kernel extracts local features</td><td>Weight sharing — orders of magnitude fewer parameters than a dense layer on images</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Pooling</b></td><td>Downsamples (max / avg / strided conv)</td><td>Grows receptive field, adds robustness to small shifts</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>BatchNorm</b></td><td>Normalizes activations per mini-batch</td><td>Stabilizes training, allows higher learning rates</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Residual skip</b></td><td><code>y = F(x) + x</code> (ResNet)</td><td>Gradients flow unimpeded → networks with 100+ layers</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Global avg pool</b></td><td>Collapses spatial dims to a vector</td><td>Bridges the visual stack to a classification head</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <div class="callout key">
    <span class="ct">Lineage worth knowing</span>
    <p>LeNet (1998) → AlexNet (2012) → VGG / GoogLeNet / ResNet (2014–15) → EfficientNet (2019). Each step was less about a new idea and more about training a bigger one <em>reliably</em>: better initialization, normalization, and skip connections did as much as any structural novelty.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>2.4 RNNs — memory as state</h2>
  <p>Recurrent networks process sequences step by step, carrying a hidden state <code>hₜ = tanh(W·hₜ₋₁ + U·xₜ)</code>. Elegant, but the same matrix is multiplied <em>t</em> times, so gradients shrink or explode along the path. <strong>LSTMs</strong> added a gated cell state that can hold information across long spans; <strong>GRUs</strong> simplified the gate structure. For short, tightly local sequences they remain light and effective; for long-range dependencies, attention won.</p>

  <h2>2.5 Attention and transformers</h2>
  <p>The transformer replaced recurrence with <strong>attention</strong>: every position can read every other position directly, in parallel. The mechanism is three learned projections — Query, Key, Value — compared pairwise:</p>
<pre><span class="cm"># multi-head attention in four lines</span>
Q, K, V = Wq x, Wk x, Wv x              <span class="cm"># projections</span>
scores  = Q @ K.T / sqrt(d_k)           <span class="cm"># similarity matrix</span>
attn    = softmax(scores, dim=-1)       <span class="cm"># weight the values</span>
out     = attn @ V                      <span class="cm"># weighted combination</span></pre>
  <p>The encoder block assembles the pieces: <strong>input + positional encoding</strong> → <strong>self-attention</strong> (every token reads all tokens) → a <strong>two-layer feedforward net</strong> applied per position → <strong>residual connections + LayerNorm</strong> for stable gradients → repeat for <code>N</code> layers → task head. Stack <code>N</code> of them, scale the dimension <code>d</code>, and you get the same machine that powers modern language, vision, and multimodal models. The architecture has been stable since 2017; the <em>scale and data</em> are what changed.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Encoder-only</strong> (BERT-style): bidirectional context → understanding tasks, embeddings, classification.</li>
    <li><strong>Decoder-only</strong> (GPT-style): causal mask, next-token prediction → the dominant architecture for generative LLMs.</li>
    <li><strong>Encoder–decoder</strong> (T5 / the original): read with an encoder, generate with a decoder → translation and summarization.</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="callout key">
    <span class="ct">Why attention scaled</span>
    <p>Recurrence forces a serial chain of length <code>t</code>; attention computes all pairwise relations at once and parallelizes perfectly on GPUs. The cost is <code>O(t²)</code> memory — which is why modern work keeps reinventing efficient attention (sliding windows, sparsity, linear approximations) rather than abandoning it.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>2.6 Generative models</h2>
  <p>A distinct family of architectures aims not to classify but to <em>produce</em> — images, audio, text. Four mechanisms dominate:</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Family</th><th>Core idea</th><th>Trade-off</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>GANs</b></td><td>A generator and discriminator play a min-max game until fakes become indistinguishable from real</td><td>Powerful but notoriously unstable; mode collapse is the classic failure</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>VAEs</b></td><td>Match an encoder's output to a Gaussian prior, learning a smooth probabilistic latent space</td><td>Softer samples, but the most principled latent geometry — and a natural regularizer</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Diffusion</b></td><td>Noisify data to pure noise, then train a network to reverse the process; iterate the reversal to sample</td><td>The current king of high-fidelity image synthesis; slower at inference</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Autoregressive</b></td><td>Model <code>P(x) = Π P(xᵢ | x&lt;ᵢ)</code>: predict the next element, append it, repeat</td><td>Simple and flexible — via the transformer decoder, the engine of modern text generation</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>2.7 Ensembles and tree methods</h2>
  <p>Not everything is a neural net. <strong>Random forests</strong> average many decorrelated trees (bagging); <strong>gradient boosting</strong> (XGBoost, LightGBM) sequences trees, each correcting its predecessor's residuals. On tabular data they remain a formidable baseline — often beating neural networks with a fraction of the engineering. A useful rule: a model that a well-tuned ensemble cannot improve is a model you can trust.</p>

  <h2>2.8 Choosing an architecture — a decision rubric</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>If…</th><th>Then lean toward</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>What modality?</b></td><td>Images / video</td><td>CNN, ViT, diffusion</td></tr>
      <tr><td></td><td>Text / language</td><td>Transformer (decoder-only for generation)</td></tr>
      <tr><td></td><td>Tabular data</td><td>Boosted trees first, MLP second</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>How much data?</b></td><td>Under ~10⁴ labeled rows</td><td>Simple models + strong features; deep nets overfit</td></tr>
      <tr><td></td><td>10⁶+ samples or tokens</td><td>Deep networks and pretraining pay off</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Latency budget?</b></td><td>Milliseconds, edge device</td><td>Small CNN, distilled model, or trees</td></tr>
      <tr><td></td><td>Server-side, batched</td><td>Big transformers are affordable</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Interpretability?</b></td><td>Required for compliance</td><td>Linear models or trees; probe-based explanations for nets</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="exercise">
    <span class="etag">Exercise 02</span>
    <ol>
      <li>Train a 2-layer MLP and a 6-layer MLP (with residual connections) on MNIST. Plot train and validation loss for both.</li>
      <li>Remove the nonlinearity from the 6-layer net. Watch what happens — and explain <em>why</em> in one sentence.</li>
      <li>Now compare gradient norms across layers with and without the skip connection. What did the residual do?</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

