<section class="part chapter" id="ch3">
  <p class="chap-kicker">Chapter 3</p>
  <h1 class="chap-title">Data Preparation</h1>
  <p class="chap-lede">Data preparation is the real model. There is a quiet law of the field: models commoditize, data doesn't.</p>

  <h2>3.1 The seven-stage pipeline</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Collection and sourcing.</strong> Define the target population precisely; data collected outside it is confidently wrong. Document provenance — you will need it for audits.</li>
    <li><strong>Cleaning and deduplication.</strong> Fix malformed records, collapse exact and near-duplicates, reconcile units and timezones. Duplicates are silent label-leakage factories.</li>
    <li><strong>Labeling.</strong> The most expensive stage. Write a labeling rubric <em>before</em> hiring labelers, compute inter-annotator agreement (Cohen's κ or Krippendorff's α), and route disagreements to adjudication. Your label noise sets the ceiling on achievable accuracy — no model escapes it.</li>
    <li><strong>Feature engineering.</strong> Encoding (one-hot, target, embeddings), scaling, aggregations, windowed statistics for sequences. For text: tokenization and vocabulary design. For images: resizing, crops, color space.</li>
    <li><strong>Splitting.</strong> Hold out validation and test sets <em>before</em> any modeling decision. For temporal data: split by time, never randomly. For grouped data: split by group, or you leak.</li>
    <li><strong>Balancing and weighting.</strong> Address class imbalance with stratification, resampling, or loss weighting — and decide whether the model should optimize accuracy or cost (Chapter 4 explains why these differ).</li>
    <li><strong>Packaging and versioning.</strong> Ship datasets as versioned artifacts (DVC, LakeFS, HF Datasets) with a schema, a hash, and a changelog. Reproducibility is a data problem before it is a code problem.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Where does the time actually go? In a typical industrial project, by engineer-hours:</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Share of effort</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Labeling</td><td>≈ 44%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cleaning</td><td>≈ 21%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>QA and audit</td><td>≈ 14%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Splitting</td><td>≈ 9%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Packaging</td><td>≈ 7%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Sourcing</td><td>≈ 5%</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Training itself is rarely the bottleneck.</p>

  <h2>3.2 The preprocessing patterns that repeat everywhere</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Standard treatment</th><th>Classic failure</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>Missing values</b></td><td>Impute (median / model-based) or add explicit missing-indicator features</td><td>Imputing on the full set <em>before</em> splitting leaks test information into train</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Scale mismatch</b></td><td>Standardize / min-max, <em>fitted on train only</em>, applied to val/test</td><td>Scaling on all data — a subtle, pervasive leak</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>High-cardinality categoricals</b></td><td>Target encoding (with smoothing / CV) or learned embeddings</td><td>Naive target encoding overfits; one-hot explodes dimensionality</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Class imbalance</b></td><td>Stratified splits, class weights, focal loss, targeted resampling</td><td>Random resampling duplicates patterns and inflates confidence</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Temporal data</b></td><td>Time-ordered splits, embargo gaps, walk-forward validation</td><td>Random splits that let the model peek at "future" rows</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Text</b></td><td>Tokenization, vocabulary / BPE, truncation or windowing</td><td>Training and inference using different tokenizers</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Images</b></td><td>Resize + random crops/flips (train), center crop (test); augmentation</td><td>Applying test-time transforms only at train time</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <div class="callout key">
    <span class="ct">The cardinal rule</span>
    <p>Every statistic you compute — means, frequencies, encodings, scaler parameters — must be derived from <strong>training data only</strong>, then applied to validation and test. Fit on the fold, transform everything. Frameworks like scikit-learn's <code>Pipeline</code> and <code>cross_val_score</code> exist to make this hard to get wrong. Use them.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>3.3 Data quality: the audits that save you</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Distribution drift check.</strong> Compare feature distributions across time slices; a silent shift in production is the most common cause of "the model degraded overnight."</li>
    <li><strong>Label audit.</strong> Sample 200 labeled examples, re-label blind, measure agreement. Below κ ≈ 0.6, fix the rubric, not the model.</li>
    <li><strong>Nearest-neighbor dedup.</strong> Embedding-based kNN over the corpus to surface near-duplicates across train/val/test boundaries.</li>
    <li><strong>Outlier profiling.</strong> Isolation Forest or per-feature z-score review; decide per feature whether to clip, keep, or drop.</li>
    <li><strong>Representation check.</strong> Train a small probe on the frozen features; if the probe cannot separate the classes, the features (or the labels) are broken.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>3.4 The ethics layer</h2>
  <p>Preparation is also where bias is <em>introduced or prevented</em>. Four questions before a dataset ships:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Who is missing?</strong> Coverage across demographics, regions, and languages — absence in data is absence in service.</li>
    <li><strong>Whose labels?</strong> Labeler demographics and rubric framing shape what the model considers "normal."</li>
    <li><strong>Consent and rights.</strong> Is the source licensed, is PII redacted, does the data contain biometric or health information with special legal status?</li>
    <li><strong>Harm surface.</strong> Who bears the cost when this model is wrong, and is that cost symmetric across groups? Run a group-wise fairness slice (Chapter 4) before launch.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="exercise">
    <span class="etag">Exercise 03</span>
    <ol>
      <li>Take any tabular dataset with &gt;5% missingness. Fit a scaler on the <em>full</em> dataset, then fit it on train only. Compare test RMSE between the two pipelines.</li>
      <li>Deliberately create a temporal leak: shuffle a time-series dataset and split randomly. Watch validation performance look "too good."</li>
      <li>Fix it with a time-ordered split and an 11-day embargo. Quantify how much of the "performance" was leakage.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- PART:CH4 -->
<section class="part chapter" id="ch4">
  <p class="chap-kicker">Chapter 4</p>
  <h1 class="chap-title">Metrics</h1>
  <p class="chap-lede">What you measure is what you get. A metric is a contract between the model and the business — pick the wrong one and the optimizer will find the most efficient way to fail your actual goal.</p>

  <h2>4.1 Classification, dissected</h2>
  <p>Every classification evaluation starts from the <strong>confusion matrix</strong>: <code>TP</code> true positives, <code>FP</code> false positives, <code>TN</code> true negatives, <code>FN</code> false negatives. From those four cells:</p>
<pre>accuracy = (TP + TN) / N             <span class="cm"># overall correctness — useless when classes are rare</span>
precision =  TP / (TP + FP)          <span class="cm"># of what you flagged, how much was real? (cost of false alarms)</span>
recall     =  TP / (TP + FN)         <span class="cm"># of what was real, how much did you catch? (cost of misses)</span>
F1         =  2·P·R / (P + R)        <span class="cm"># harmonic mean — punishes lopsidedness</span>
Fβ         =  (1+β²)·P·R / (β²·P + R) <span class="cm"># β&gt;1 weights recall; β&lt;1 weights precision</span></pre>
  <p>On an imbalanced dataset (fraud: 0.2% positive), a model that flags <em>nothing</em> scores 99.8% accuracy and catches nothing. <strong>Recall and precision are the honest currencies</strong>; F1 and Fβ let you encode the trade-off numerically — F2 says "missing cases hurt twice as much as false alarms."</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Scenario</th><th>Priority</th><th>Lead metric</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>Fraud / spam detection</b></td><td>Don't miss real fraud; false alarms tolerable</td><td>Recall at fixed precision, PR-AUC</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Medical screening</b></td><td>Missing disease is catastrophic</td><td>Sensitivity (recall) at high specificity</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Ad click prediction</b></td><td>Flags must be trustworthy</td><td>Precision, top-k lift</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Balanced multiclass</b></td><td>Even performance across classes</td><td>Macro-averaged F1</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Rare-class multiclass</b></td><td>Don't let the majority drown the minority</td><td>Weighted F1 / per-class recall report</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>4.2 Ranking: ROC and PR curves</h2>
  <p>Real classifiers emit <em>probabilities</em>, and a threshold is a business decision — so evaluate the whole curve, not one cut point. <strong>ROC-AUC</strong> plots true-positive rate against false-positive rate across all thresholds; it measures the probability that a random positive ranks above a random negative. Under extreme imbalance it looks flattering, because the FPR denominator is huge. <strong>PR-AUC</strong> plots precision against recall and is the correct lens when positives are rare — it answers "when I flag <em>this many</em>, how many are real?"</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Use ROC-AUC when</strong> classes are reasonably balanced and you need a threshold-free summary. AUC = 0.5 is a coin; 0.9+ is strong; report the confidence interval, not just the point.</li>
    <li><strong>Use PR-AUC when</strong> positives are rare (under ~5%) or the cost asymmetry is extreme. A model can post great ROC-AUC and still be practically useless at the operating point you deploy at.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>4.3 Regression metrics</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>Formula / idea</th><th>Best for</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>MSE</b></td><td><code>mean((y−ŷ)²)</code></td><td>Default differentiable loss; punishes large errors hard</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>RMSE</b></td><td><code>√MSE</code></td><td>Reporting in target units; same penalty profile as MSE</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>MAE</b></td><td><code>mean(|y−ŷ|)</code></td><td>Robustness to outliers; straight-line interpretation</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Huber</b></td><td>MSE near zero, MAE in the tails</td><td>Noisy labels — MSE's gradient with MAE's robustness</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Quantile loss</b></td><td>Asymmetric penalty per quantile τ</td><td>Prediction intervals, "95th percentile of delivery time"</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Always pair a point metric with an <em>error distribution</em>: mean error (bias), a histogram of residuals, and a Q–Q plot. A model with zero mean error can still be systematically wrong in the tails, where the money is.</p>

  <h2>4.4 Language and generation metrics</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Perplexity</strong> — <code>exp(−log-likelihood)</code>; the model's effective branching factor. Excellent for tracking pretraining health; weak as a proxy for task quality.</li>
    <li><strong>BLEU / ROUGE</strong> — n-gram overlap with references. Fast and cheap, but they reward fluent <em>generic</em> text over correct specific text. Use for regression monitoring, not go/no-go decisions.</li>
    <li><strong>BERTScore</strong> — embedding similarity between generated and reference tokens. More tolerant of paraphrase; still reference-bound.</li>
    <li><strong>LLM-as-judge</strong> — a strong model grades outputs against a rubric. The current standard for open-ended quality, with real caveats: self-preference bias, positional bias, and rubric drift. Calibrate against human scores on a held-out set.</li>
    <li><strong>Task metrics</strong> — for grounded tasks (code, math, extraction), prefer execution pass rate, exact set match, or schema validation. Verifiable beats vibes.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>4.5 Fairness and calibration</h2>
  <p>A global metric can hide a failing subgroup. Report your lead metric <em>per slice</em> — by demographic group, region, device, or traffic source — and set launch gates on the worst slice, not the mean. Complement this with <strong>calibration</strong>: if the model says 80%, do 80% of those cases actually occur? A reliability diagram (predicted probability vs. observed frequency) exposes overconfidence; temperature scaling and isotonic regression fix it post-hoc at near-zero cost.</p>

  <h2>4.6 Feeling the precision–recall trade-off</h2>
  <p>Imagine a fraud detector facing <strong>900 true frauds among 99,100 legitimate transactions</strong> (0.9% positive rate). The table below sweeps the decision threshold over a synthetic score distribution and shows what "better" actually means when the classes are lopsided:</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Threshold</th><th>Recall</th><th>Precision</th><th>Catches (TP)</th><th>False alarms (FP)</th><th>F1</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>0.30</td><td>90.9%</td><td>1.6%</td><td>818</td><td>49,550</td><td>0.032</td></tr>
      <tr><td>0.40</td><td>82.1%</td><td>2.4%</td><td>739</td><td>30,572</td><td>0.046</td></tr>
      <tr><td>0.50</td><td>69.1%</td><td>3.8%</td><td>622</td><td>15,727</td><td>0.072</td></tr>
      <tr><td>0.60</td><td>53.3%</td><td>6.8%</td><td>480</td><td>6,620</td><td>0.120</td></tr>
      <tr><td>0.70</td><td>36.9%</td><td>12.8%</td><td>332</td><td>2,255</td><td>0.190</td></tr>
      <tr><td>0.80</td><td>22.7%</td><td>24.9%</td><td>204</td><td>615</td><td>0.237</td></tr>
      <tr><td>0.90</td><td>12.2%</td><td>45.1%</td><td>110</td><td>134</td><td>0.192</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The left side (t ≤ 0.40)</strong> is maximum recall: you catch most fraud, but the false-alarm column is the story — at 0.30 you flag 49,550 clean transactions to stop 818 frauds.</li>
    <li><strong>The middle (0.50–0.70)</strong> is where operating decisions actually happen: recall falls from about 69% to 37%, while precision climbs from 4% to 13%.</li>
    <li><strong>The right side (t ≥ 0.80)</strong> buys trust at the price of coverage: at 0.90 a flagged case is real 45% of the time, yet you still miss 88% of fraud.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>No single threshold wins; the right one is defined by your cost function — the price of a missed fraud versus the price of a wrongly flagged customer. (Numbers derive from synthetic score distributions — positives ≈ N(0.62, 0.24), negatives ≈ N(0.30, 0.20). The shape is the point, not the digits.)</p>

  <div class="exercise">
    <span class="etag">Exercise 04</span>
    <ol>
      <li>On an imbalanced dataset, compute accuracy, ROC-AUC, and PR-AUC for a model that always predicts the majority class. Note what each metric claims.</li>
      <li>Plot the precision-recall curve for your real model and find the threshold that maximizes F2.</li>
      <li>Report your lead metric on three slices you define (e.g., by sign-up channel). Which slice fails the launch gate?</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- PART:CH5 -->
<section class="part chapter" id="ch5">
  <p class="chap-kicker">Chapter 5</p>
  <h1 class="chap-title">Training Methods</h1>
  <p class="chap-lede">Optimization: walking a mountain range in the dark. The loss surface of a modern network is non-convex, wildly scaled, and studded with saddle points — training is the art of still finding a good valley.</p>

  <h2>5.1 Gradient descent and its descendants</h2>
  <p><strong>Batch GD</strong> computes the exact gradient per step — accurate, but memory-heavy. <strong>SGD</strong> uses one sample — noisy, but the noise is a feature: it escapes sharp minima. <strong>Mini-batch SGD</strong> is the practical middle. Modern optimizers stack clever state on top of SGD:</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Optimizer</th><th>Idea</th><th>When to reach for it</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>Momentum</b></td><td>Accumulates a velocity term; smooths oscillations</td><td>Default upgrade over vanilla SGD for deep nets</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>AdaGrad</b></td><td>Per-parameter adaptive LR from accumulated gradients</td><td>Sparse features (early NLP); LR decays too aggressively for dense nets</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>RMSProp</b></td><td>Exponentially-decayed adaptive LR</td><td>Fixes AdaGrad's decay; classic for RNNs</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Adam</b></td><td>Momentum + RMSProp (1st and 2nd moment estimates)</td><td>The workhorse default; fast, forgiving hyperparameters</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>AdamW</b></td><td>Adam with <em>decoupled</em> weight decay</td><td>Transformers and large-scale training; the modern default</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>SGD + momentum</b></td><td>The classic, tuned carefully</td><td>Often edges out Adam on final accuracy for CV tasks if you have the tuning budget</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>5.2 Learning rate: the number that matters most</h2>
  <p>No hyperparameter changes outcomes as much as the learning rate. Too high and the loss diverges or oscillates forever; too low and you crawl. The modern playbook:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>LR range test.</strong> Sweep log-spaced LRs for a few hundred steps; pick the highest rate whose loss is still descending — the "no-divergence point," often a hair below the minimum observed loss.</li>
    <li><strong>Warmup.</strong> Start small (1–5% of peak) and ramp linearly; stabilizes early training while normalization statistics are still settling.</li>
    <li><strong>Scheduled decay.</strong> Cosine annealing to a low floor is the transformer standard; step decay remains common in CV.</li>
    <li><strong>Batch-size coupling.</strong> Larger batches permit higher LRs (linear scaling rule), but reduce the beneficial noise — watch generalization.</li>
    <li><strong>Gradient clipping.</strong> Clip the global norm (typically to 1.0); cheap insurance against explosion, especially in RNNs and early transformer layers.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>5.3 Regularization: the anti-overfit toolkit</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Technique</th><th>What it does</th><th>When to use it</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>Augmentation</b></td><td>Crops, flips, color jitter, MixUp, cutmix; synonym dropout for text; controlled noise for tables</td><td>The strongest regularizer in vision — if the validation gap is your problem, augment harder before touching the model</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Weight decay and dropout</b></td><td>Decoupled L2 shrinks weights and smooths the landscape; dropout mutes units each step, forcing redundancy</td><td>Both cheap, both effective, both worth tuning — don't leave them at defaults forever</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Early stopping and ensembling</b></td><td>Stop when validation loss plateaus (free regularization with built-in model selection); ensemble seeds or architectures</td><td>Ensembling is the highest-leverage "last 2%" before you ship</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Architectural and data-based</b></td><td>Smaller capacity, depthwise separable convs, distillation from a big teacher into a small student</td><td>And the quiet giant: <em>more, better data</em> beats most regularization tricks — spend there first</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>5.4 Diagnosing the two classic failures</h2>
  <div class="fail good">
    <h3>Underfitting — the model is too weak</h3>
    <p>Train and validation loss are both high and still descending: the model hasn't learned the structure. <strong>Fixes:</strong> more capacity, better features, longer training, a higher learning rate. Check for bugs first — a loss that can't move is often a dead activation or a forgotten <code>optimizer.step()</code>.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="fail bad">
    <h3>Overfitting — the model is memorizing</h3>
    <p>Train loss keeps falling while validation loss turns upward: the model is fitting noise. <strong>Fixes:</strong> more data, augmentation, dropout, weight decay, early stopping, a smaller model. If the gap is huge, suspect label noise or leakage before anything else.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>5.5 Pretraining and fine-tuning</h2>
  <p>The modern training stack is layered. <strong>Pretraining</strong> learns general representations from massive unlabeled (or self-supervised) data — next-token prediction for text, masked modeling for encoders, denoising for diffusion. <strong>Fine-tuning</strong> adapts those weights to your task with far less data. <strong>Parameter-efficient variants</strong> — LoRA (low-rank adapters), prefix tuning, adapters — update a small fraction of the weights and make large-model adaptation practical on a single GPU. The rule of thumb: freeze the backbone, tune the head, add LoRA only when you need the capacity. And <strong>RLHF / DPO</strong> layers preference learning on top — for behavior, not just accuracy.</p>

  <h2>5.6 Scaling, hardware, and the practicalities of big training</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Mixed precision (FP16 / BF16).</strong> Halves memory and roughly doubles throughput; BF16 is the safe default for large transformers, with loss scaling for FP16.</li>
    <li><strong>Gradient accumulation.</strong> Simulate a larger batch when the GPU can't hold one; mathematically equivalent (modulo batch-norm nuances).</li>
    <li><strong>Distributed strategies.</strong> Data parallelism (shard batches), tensor parallelism (shard layers), pipeline parallelism (shard stages), and ZeRO / FSDP (shard optimizer state and parameters). Choose by where your memory bottleneck is.</li>
    <li><strong>Checkpointing and fault tolerance.</strong> At scale, hardware <em>will</em> fail mid-run. Async checkpoints, elastic re-launch, and deterministic replay are part of the architecture, not an afterthought.</li>
    <li><strong>Scaling laws.</strong> Loss scales as a power law in model size, dataset size, and compute (Chinchilla: match tokens to parameters at roughly 20:1). Use them to decide the shape of your budget before you spend it.</li>
    <li><strong>Reproducibility.</strong> Pin seeds, log everything (W&amp;B / MLflow), version data and code. A run you can't reproduce is a run you can't defend.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="exercise">
    <span class="etag">Exercise 05</span>
    <ol>
      <li>Run an LR range test on a small CNN. Plot loss vs. LR and identify the no-divergence point.</li>
      <li>Train the same net with and without warmup plus cosine decay. Measure final validation loss and the steps to reach 90% of the way there.</li>
      <li>Introduce deliberate overfitting (tiny batch, high LR, no decay). Then fix it one technique at a time and record which single change closed the train/validation gap the most.</li>
    </ol>
