<!-- PART:CH6 -->
<section class="part chapter" id="ch6">
  <p class="chap-kicker">Chapter 6</p>
  <h1 class="chap-title">Beyond Training</h1>
  <p class="chap-lede">A model isn't finished when it trains. It's finished when it's trusted. The final mile is where most projects actually die — not in the loss curve, but in the deployment, the drift, the stakeholder meeting, and the 2 a.m. incident.</p>

  <h2>6.1 Evaluation and model selection</h2>
  <p>Lock the test set. Run the full metric suite — lead metric, slices, calibration, latency, cost. Select the checkpoint by validation performance <em>only</em>, then report the test set once. If you look at the test set repeatedly, it becomes validation; that's why careful teams keep a "gold" set that is scored exactly twice, ever. Define the launch gates before the run, not after.</p>

  <h2>6.2 Optimization and packaging</h2>
  <p>Export to ONNX or a runtime format, quantize (INT8 / INT4) where accuracy holds, prune or distill if the serving budget is tight, and bake the preprocessing into the artifact. The model and its transforms ship as one unit — a model file without its scaler is a promise without a contract.</p>

  <h2>6.3 Serving and inference</h2>
  <p>Choose the topology: REST for simple requests, streaming for generation, batch for offline scoring. Batch aggressively — GPU utilization scales with batch size until latency dies. Set timeouts, rate limits, and a graceful fallback (cached answer, smaller model, human queue) for when the fast path fails.</p>

  <h2>6.4 Monitoring and drift</h2>
  <p>Track three families of signals: <em>input drift</em> (feature distributions vs. training), <em>prediction drift</em> (score distribution over time), and <em>outcome drift</em> (do labels still arrive, and does the model still predict them well?). Alert on PSI or KL divergence per feature; retraining triggers should be defined in advance, not invented in the incident channel.</p>

  <h2>6.5 Retraining and lifecycle</h2>
  <p>Schedule retraining on data freshness, not calendar convenience. Version every artifact — model, data, code, config — as a single deployable unit. Keep the last known-good checkpoint hot for instant rollback, and automate the champion/challenger comparison so a new model must <em>beat</em> the old one on the live eval set before it earns production.</p>

  <h2>6.6 Governance and continuous audit</h2>
  <p>Maintain the model card: intended use, known limitations, slice performance, and a named rollback owner. Run periodic fairness and safety audits, especially after any data or model change. Treat the model as a regulated surface — the same review rigor you'd give a payment system, scaled to its actual blast radius.</p>

  <div class="callout key">
    <span class="ct">The MLOps loop, in one sentence</span>
    <p>Capture → curate → train → evaluate → deploy → monitor → detect drift → re-capture. The loop, not the model, is the product.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="exercise">
    <span class="etag">Exercise 06</span>
    <ol>
      <li>Wrap your trained model in a FastAPI endpoint with a request validator and a 200 ms p95 budget. Load-test it and find where the bottleneck actually is.</li>
      <li>Compute PSI between your training features and the last 7 days of (simulated) production features. Which feature drifted most?</li>
      <li>Write the model card for your system in under 300 words. You will be amazed what you don't yet know about it.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

