<section class="part" id="glossary">
  <h1 class="part-title">Glossary</h1>
  <dl class="gloss">
    <dt>Backpropagation</dt><dd>Application of the chain rule that computes the gradient of the loss with respect to every parameter in a single backward pass.</dd>
    <dt>Batch size</dt><dd>The number of examples processed per optimizer step; interacts with learning rate, memory, and generalization.</dd>
    <dt>Causal masking</dt><dd>The decoder-only trick of letting a position attend only to earlier positions, making next-token prediction well-defined.</dd>
    <dt>Class imbalance</dt><dd>One label far rarer than the others, making accuracy misleading and precision/recall the honest metrics.</dd>
    <dt>Data leakage</dt><dd>Information from validation/test (or the future) sneaking into training and inflating scores; the most common silent failure.</dd>
    <dt>Drift</dt><dd>Live data distributions shifting away from training distributions, quietly degrading performance.</dd>
    <dt>Embedding</dt><dd>A learned dense vector representation of a discrete item — a word, a user, an image patch.</dd>
    <dt>Epoch</dt><dd>One full pass over the training data.</dd>
    <dt>Fine-tuning</dt><dd>Adapting pretrained weights to a task with far less data.</dd>
    <dt>Gradient</dt><dd>The direction of steepest ascent of the loss; optimizers step against it.</dd>
    <dt>Inference</dt><dd>Running a trained model on new inputs — the latency and cost stage.</dd>
    <dt>Overfitting</dt><dd>Fitting noise: training error falls while validation error rises.</dd>
    <dt>Perplexity</dt><dd><code>exp(−average log-likelihood)</code>; a language model's effective branching factor.</dd>
    <dt>Regularization</dt><dd>Any constraint that fights overfitting: weight decay, dropout, augmentation, early stopping, capacity limits.</dd>
    <dt>Residual connection</dt><dd>A skip link, <code>y = F(x) + x</code>, that lets gradients flow through deep networks.</dd>
    <dt>Warmup</dt><dd>The short opening phase of training in which the learning rate ramps up from a small value.</dd>
  </dl>
